  "query_preview": "..\n\nHealthy atmospheric moisture will remain across southeastern \nArizona today with surface dew points of 55-60 degrees and\n1.2-1.6 inches of precipitable water. Impulses aloft continue to\npush into the region through east/southeast flow, which will\ntrigger scattered thunderstorms across the region again this\nafternoon. The main hazard today will be heavy rainfall rates \nand flash flooding, with an isolated strong wind gust threat. \nWeak wind shear should preclude an organized thunderstorm wind \nthreat but enough mid- level drying has shown up in forecast \nsoundings to indicate that a strong downburst may accompany any\nthunderstorm today. Mean wind will begin to turn northeasterly \nthrough the day with thunderstorm motions taking on a northeast \nto southwest to west motion.\n\nThe active pattern continues through the week, becoming enhanced\nduring the second half of the week by an incoming easterly wave.\nEnsembles are in good agreement with increasing perceptible \nwater overlapping with lowering heights aloft Thursday and\nFriday, ingredients highlighting an elevated heavy rainfall\npotential. There remain details to work out regarding \ninstability and rainfall rates, but this period will need to be \nwatched for more widespread impacts. Currently the WPC has \nThursday and Friday in a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall \nacross the region. \n\nHigh temperatures will likely trend down with this incoming\neasterly wave with forecast highs dipping from around 3 degrees\nbelow normal through Wednesday to around 10 degrees below \nnormal on Friday. An extended period of rain and cloud cover\nduring the day may bring even lower highs.",
